INT. HOUSEBOAT GALLEY, CANAL - SUNDAY NOON

Steam on the windows. A pot of stew on a two-ring stove. The deck rocks gently. A table meant for four holds five.

INES VASCONCELOS (70s, apron, flour to the elbows) stirs. Her son KOFI VASCONCELOS-MENSAH (40s, glasses) carves bread at the counter. His sister BRIGITTE VASCONCELOS (40s, silk scarf) sets spoons. Two kids tumble in from the deck: LUCAS (9, muddy knees) and ZANELE (7, missing a front tooth).

INES
Everybody sit. Everybody. The stew is hot.

LUCAS
Nonna, I'm not hungry.

ZANELE
I am!

KOFI
Wash hands first.

BRIGITTE
Both of them.

LUCAS
We did it in the canal.

BRIGITTE
That is not washing.

ZANELE
It had a duck in it.

KOFI
(muttering)
Of course it did.

She holds up two spoons. He shakes his head. She sets one down. He takes the other.

INES
Kofi, did you speak to the landlord?

KOFI
Mama, not at the table.

INES
Everything is at the table. That is what it is for.

BRIGITTE
He said it's fine, Mama. The mooring is paid till spring.

KOFI
I was going to say that.

BRIGITTE
You were going to say it badly.

LUCAS
Can I have the end piece?

ZANELE
No, I want the end piece!

INES
Both of you can have half.

ZANELE
Lucas always gets the big half.

LUCAS
Do not.

KOFI
(to the whole room)
Okay. Everyone. Quiet for one second.

They hush. The boat creaks. Somewhere a gull complains.

KOFI (CONT'D)
I'd like to say grace.

INES
Since when do you say grace?

KOFI
Since now. Since the mooring is paid and the stew is hot and nobody fell in the canal this week.

ZANELE
I fell in the canal.

KOFI
Nobody fell in the canal that I saw.

ZANELE
Okay.

BRIGITTE
Say the grace, Kofi.

He lowers his head. They all lower theirs, except Zanele, who peeks. Lucas peeks too. They catch each other peeking and giggle.

KOFI (CONT'D)
Thank you for this boat, and this stew, and this bread, and these people.

INES
Amen.

BRIGITTE
Amen.

LUCAS
Amen. Can I eat now?

ZANELE
Amen! Can I eat now?

INES
Eat, eat. Slowly.

They tuck in. For a while there is only the scrape of spoons and the slosh of water against the hull. Brigitte tears a piece of bread and hands it to Ines, who tastes the stew, frowns, and reaches for salt.

INES (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Always needs a little more.

KOFI
Mama, it's perfect.

INES
Perfect is a lie we tell cooks.

BRIGITTE
Then we're liars. Pass the salt, Zanele.

ZANELE
It's by Lucas.

LUCAS
Here.

He slides the salt across. Ines salts the stew, tastes it again, and nods, and then, with a small private smile, passes the salt back to Zanele.
